 "report": "Particle A42-spiral-e000-d010: inhibitory knot (k=4)\n\nWhat it is: a particle of mass 4.6 and radius 1.28 with 4 ports on its rim. Each port is a stick-slip contact with a dead zone (mean 0.43) and a 4-dim charge; 2 of its port pairs attract each other strongly (so copies can bind port to port). Inside, 10 couplings (100% inhibitory) pass a push from port to port; it settles in 3 sweeps with slip rate 0.51 and keeps its state (memory).\nHow it assembles (32 copies, 2 worlds): 0.50 bonds per particle, largest molecule 3.5 particles, 17.5 left free.\n\nIts matter vs an ESN of the same size (readout only, both with tuned input scale); controls: no bonds, random bonds:\n  memcap   0.101 vs ESN 0.998\u00b10.001  [below]  no bonds 0.104  random bonds 0.098\n  xor      0.549 vs ESN 0.627\u00b10.259  [match, at chance]  no bonds 0.501  random bonds 0.591\n  narma10  0.366 vs ESN 0.836\u00b10.001  [below]  no bonds 0.395  random bonds 0.179\n  latch    0.888 vs ESN 0.674\u00b10.004  [beat]  no bonds 0.884  random bonds 0.888\n  mnist14  0.572 vs ESN 0.776\u00b10.020  [below]  no bonds 0.458  random bonds 0.474\n\nGood at: latch.\nIts assembly does not matter: the particles do as well unbonded, so the useful part is the single particle's own stick-slip memory.\nUse (a guess): holding a value until it is overwritten, with nothing trained but the readout: a physical memory element for a recurrent or reservoir system."
